WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.441
The Southern California Planning Model is an integrated framework developed for regional analysis.

2
00:00:05.441 --> 00:00:13.937
The source chapter presents it as a way to connect economic and demographic change, land development, location, and transportation across space and time.

3
00:00:13.937 --> 00:00:22.043
A regional model is valuable because housing markets, employment, commuting, infrastructure, and environmental effects cross municipal boundaries.

4
00:00:22.043 --> 00:00:27.818
Integrated regional models often begin with forecasts or scenarios for population and economic activity.

5
00:00:27.818 --> 00:00:38.367
They represent land supply and constraints, allocate households and employment, calculate access through the transportation network, and update development or location over multiple periods.

6
00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:43.031
Some versions can test hazards, disruptions, or recovery as well as ordinary growth.

7
00:00:43.031 --> 00:00:44.586
The scale creates tradeoffs.

8
00:00:44.586 --> 00:00:49.694
A zone system can cover a large region but smooth over parcel conditions and local barriers.

9
00:00:49.694 --> 00:00:54.414
Detailed categories improve interpretation but increase data and calibration demands.

10
00:00:54.414 --> 00:00:57.468
Regional consistency does not guarantee local accuracy.

11
00:00:57.468 --> 00:01:03.242
Policy analysis should focus on scenario differences, not false precision in a single long-range number.

12
00:01:03.242 --> 00:01:13.292
Documentation should state what is endogenous, what is imposed, how markets and constraints are represented, and how the transportation and land-use components exchange information.

13
00:01:13.292 --> 00:01:14.520
Torrance application.

14
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:19.141
This section is a separate teaching example based on the Torrance General Plan.

15
00:01:19.141 --> 00:01:23.820
It does not use or reproduce an official Southern California Planning Model run.

16
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:33.296
Torrance belongs to a regional housing, labor, freight, and transportation system, so a local scenario would need regional inputs even when the decision is local.

17
00:01:33.296 --> 00:01:43.531
An analyst might use regional forecasts as boundary conditions, then examine how alternative Torrance land-use assumptions affect access, travel, and development distribution.

18
00:01:43.531 --> 00:01:51.545
Local refinement would need adopted policy, parcel capacity, industrial and freight conditions, transit service, and observed local data.

19
00:01:51.545 --> 00:01:57.160
The analyst should compare regional model zones with City geography and explain any downscaling.

20
00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:01.663
Scenario outputs might inform questions, but they would not determine policy.

21
00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:09.559
A local decision could depend on site constraints, community priorities, or environmental effects that a regional model cannot resolve.

22
00:02:09.559 --> 00:02:14.531
This episode offers no official forecast and no finding about future Torrance growth.

23
00:02:14.531 --> 00:02:19.620
It illustrates a responsible relationship between regional modeling and local planning.

24
00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:28.510
This episode adapts chapter seven of Transportation Land Use Modeling and Policy by Pan and Sharifi, published by Mavs Open Press under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0.

25
00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:31.786
Source figures and other third-party items are excluded.

26
00:02:31.786 --> 00:02:34.476
Torrance material is independently summarized.

27
00:02:34.476 --> 00:02:35.997
No endorsement is implied.
